      "textEn": "Meaning of the word:\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shri Shukadeva Goswami said; Tatha—After this incident; Prachetasah—Daksha; Asikanyam—Ashikni\nFrom the wife named; Anuniitah—Brought to peace; Swayambhuva—By Shri Brahma; Shashtim—Sixty; Sanjanyam Aas—Born\nFrom; Duhitruh—Daughters; Pitra-vatsalah—Dear to their father. Srisukadeva Goswami said, O King! Thereafter, upon Brahmaji's prayer, Prajapati Daksha,\nwho is called Prachetas, produced sixty daughters from the womb of his wife Asikni. All the girls loved their father very much. Meaning: After the loss of his many sons, Daksha regretted his\nignorance towards Narad Muni. Then Shri Brahma went to Daksha and advised him to produce offspring. This time Daksha was careful, therefore he produced only daughters instead of sons.\nSo that Narad could not force them to accept renunciation and make them detached.\nFor women,\nrenunciation is not made; they should obey the orders of their good husbands, because if\nthe husband is worthy of liberation, then his wife automatically attains liberation. As the scriptures say,\nA wife is one who shares in her husband's sacred duties. Therefore, a woman should\nbe devoted to her husband and obedient to him. Only then, without any different effort, she\ncan share a part in her husband's benefit. Dasha Dharmaya Kayadvashatrinav Chendave. Bhutangirah krushaswebhyo dwhe dwhe tarakshay cha aparah. 2. Meaning of the word\n\nDash—ten; Dharmaya—to the king, Dharma, that is, Yamraj; Kaya—to Kashyap; Adat—given; Dvi-Shat—double of six, and\nEka—thirteen; Tri-Nav—three times nine, that is, twenty-seven; Cha—also; Indave—to Chandradeva; Bhuta-Angirah—to Bhutas,\nAngirah and Krishasvaibhyah—to Bhutas,\nDve—two, two; Tarakshaya—again to Kashyap; Cha—and; Aparah—remaining.\n.\n\nHe gave ten to Dharmaraj (Yamaraj), thirteen to Kashyap, twenty-seven to Chandrama, and two each to Angira,\nKrishashva, and Bhuta as a donation. The remaining four daughters were given to Kashyap.\nThey went (Thu