      "textEn": "Listen to me, O Namadeya, to the praises of the gods and the best of men. Yasam prasutivadarloka aapuritastrayah. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nNamadeyani—different names; Amusham—them; Tvam—you; Sa-apatyanaam—with your children; Cha—and; Me—from me;\nShrunu—Please listen; Yasam—to all of them; Prasuti-prasutaivi—by many children and descendants; Lokah—all\nLokah—are inhabited; Apuritaah—are inhabited; Trayah—three (upper, middle and lower worlds). Now, listen to the names of all these maidens and their descendants, from whom these three worlds\nare filled. Bhanurlamba Kakudyamir Vishwa Sadhya Marutvati. Vasuramuhurta Sankalpa Dharmapatyaah Sutan Shrunu. 4. Meaning of the words:\n\nBhanuha - Bhanu; Lamba - Lamba; Kakut - Kakuda; Yaamiha - Yaami; Vishva - Vishva; Sadhya - Sadhya; Marutvati - Marutvati;\nVasuha - Vasu; Muhurta - Muhurta; Sankalpa - Sankalpa; Dharma-Palya - Yamaraja's wives; Sutaan - their sons; Shrunu - Listen.\n.\nThe names of the ten daughters given to Yamraj were Bhanu, Lamba, Kakud, Yami, Vishwa, Sadhya,\n\nThe names of the ten daughters given to Yamraj were Bhanu, Lamba, Kakud, Yami, Vishwa, Sadhya,\nMarutvati, Vasu, Muhurta, and Sankalpa. Now, listen to the names of their sons. Bhanastu devarshabh rishyasandhrassatto nripa. Vidyot, the one who is the lord of the assembly, and also the one who sings the lullaby. 5. Meaning of the word\n\nBhanoah — From the womb of Bhanu; Tu – Undoubtedly; Dev-Rishabhah – Dev-Rishabh; Indrasenah – Indrasen; Tatah – From that (Dev-Rishabh);\nNrip – Oh King; Vidyot – Vidyot; Aasit – Arrived; Lambayaah – From the womb of Lamba; Tatah – From that; Cha – And;\nStanayitvavah – All the clouds.. From the womb of Bhanu, a son named Dev-Rishabh was born, who had a son named Indrasen. From the womb of Lamba, a son named Vidyot was born, who gave birth to all the clouds. Kakudaha sankatastasya kikatastanayo yatah."
