      "textEn": "Pauloma: Kalakeyaśca daṇva yuddhāśalīḥ. 35. They were six thousand sacrifices, father, father. Jaghan Swargato Rajanne Indrapriyankarah. 36. Meaning of the word\n\nVaishvanara-sutaah - daughters of Vaishvanara; Yaah - who; Cha - and; Chatasraah - four; Charu-darshanaah - extremely beautiful; Upadanvi -\nUpadanvi; Hayashira - Hayashira; Puloma - Puloma; Kalka - Kalka; Tatha - and; Upadanvim - Upadanvi;\nHiranyakshah - Asura Hiranyaksha; Kratuh - Kratu; Hayashiraam - Hayashira; Nrupa - O King; Pulomam Kalkam cha -\nPuloma and Kalka; Dve - both; Vaishvanara-sute - daughters of Vaishvanara; Tu - but; Kah - by whom; Upayeme - by marriage;\nKriya - by marriage; Atha - then; Bhagavan - the supreme power; Kashyapah - Kashyapa Muni; Brahma-choditah - from the request of Brahma;\nPaulomaah - named after Pauloma and Kalakeya; Danavaah - demons; Yuddha-shalinah - valorous in battle; The warrior; and -\nOf them; sixty thousand; Yagya-ghaan - those who destroy Yagya; Te - yours; Pitah - of father; Pita - father;\nFather; Jaghaan - killed; Swah - gone; Rajan - O King; Ekah - alone; Indra-priyam - to please; Karaah - the king Indra\nto please..\n\nDanu's son Vaishvanara had four beautiful daughters whose names were—Upadanvi, Hayashira,\nPuloma and Kalka. Among these, Hiranyaksha's marriage with Upadanvi and Kratu's with Hayashira took place. Thereafter, upon the persuasion of Shri Brahma, Prajapati Kashyap married Vaishvanara's other two daughters, Puloma and Kalka. Kashyap had sixty thousand sons from the wombs of these two\nwives, who became famous by the names of Paulom and Kalkay.\nAmong them, Nivatakavaca was prominent. They were all very brave and skilled in warfare, and their goal was\nto disrupt the sacrifices performed by the sages.\nHey Rajan! When your great-grandfather Arjun went to heaven,\nhe alone killed these demons, because of which King Indra became his most\ndear. Viprachintiha sihin kayam cha shatam cha jajjanat. Rahu, the lord, Ketu, the number, and the planetary positions that have come into effect. 37. Meaning of the word:\n\n[^1]: Viprachintiha - Viprachintine; Simhikayaam – from the womb of his wife Simhika; Shatam – one hundred; Cha – and; Ekam – one;\nAjijanat – gave birth; Rahu-Jyeshtam – among whom Rahu is the eldest; Ketu-Shatam – one hundred Ketus; Grahatvam – of being a planet;\nYe – all of them; Upagataah – attained. [^2]: Viprachiti had a hundred and one sons from his wife, Singhika, among whom Rahu was the most"
